examples.question_text:
For the polynomial graph below, state whether the degree is odd or even and whether the leading term is positive or negative.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=10mm,y=10mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[black,<->] (-3.2,0)--(3.2,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,<->] (0,-2.4)--(0,1.5) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[curveblue,thick,<->,domain=-2.6:2.6,samples=120,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{-0.18*(\x*\x-2)*(\x*\x-2)+0.7});
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
1. Determine the end behaviour.

The leading term is $x^5$. The left end points down and the right end points up.

2. Find the $y$-intercept by letting $x=0$.

$y=(2)^2(-1)^3=-4$

3. Find the $x$-intercepts by letting $y=0$.

$(x+2)^2(x-1)^3=0$, so $x=-2$ or $x=1$.

4. Identify the behaviour at each root and draw the curve.

Bounce at $x=-2$; inflect along the axis at $x=1$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=12mm,y=1mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[black,<->] (-3.2,0)--(2.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,<->] (0,-38)--(0,22) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[curveblue,thick,<->,domain=-2.8:2,samples=220,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{(\x+2)*(\x+2)*(\x-1)*(\x-1)*(\x-1)});
\fill[black] (-2,0) circle (1.3pt);
\fill[black] (1,0) circle (1.3pt);
\fill[black] (0,-4) circle (1.3pt);
\node[above] at (-2,0) {$(-2,0)$};
\node[above] at (1,0) {$(1,0)$};
\node[below right] at (0,-4) {$(0,-4)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

$\begin{aligned}&\mathrel{\phantom{=}}x^4-12x^3+36x^2\\&=x^2(x^2-12x+36)\\&=x^2(x-6)^2\end{aligned}$

1. Determine the end behaviour.

The leading term is $x^4$, so both ends point up.

2. Find the $y$-intercept by letting $x=0$.

$y=0^2(-6)^2=0$

3. Find the $x$-intercepts by letting $y=0$.

$x^2(x-6)^2=0$, so $x=0$ or $x=6$.

4. Identify the behaviour at each root and draw the curve.

Both roots are double roots, so the curve bounces at each. The product of the two squares is non-negative, so the curve stays on or above the axis.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=8mm,y=0.3mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[black,<->] (-1.8,0)--(7.8,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,<->] (0,-20)--(0,160) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[curveblue,thick,<->,domain=-1.5:7.5,samples=180,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{\x*\x*(\x-6)*(\x-6)});
\fill[black] (0,0) circle (1.3pt);
\fill[black] (6,0) circle (1.3pt);
\node[below left] at (0,0) {$(0,0)$};
\node[below] at (6,0) {$(6,0)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.question_text:
The polynomial graph below has leading coefficient $1$ or $-1$. Write its equation in factored form, using the least multiplicities consistent with the root behaviours shown.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=12mm,y=1mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[black,<->] (-2.5,0)--(3.2,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,<->] (0,-22)--(0,38) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[curveblue,thick,<->,domain=-2:2.8,samples=220,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{-(\x+1)*(\x+1)*(\x+1)*(\x-2)*(\x-2)});
\fill[black] (-1,0) circle (1.3pt);
\fill[black] (2,0) circle (1.3pt);
\node[below] at (-1,0) {$-1$};
\node[below] at (2,0) {$2$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
